00:48:33.970 --> 00:48:56.960
<v Jared Marcelle>Ian Dille was 21 when this whole thing happened. He was competing in a big bike race, a national championship. It was in Gainesville. Ian's from Austin. And when they got there, he saw that the roads were pretty flat, just like home. Plus, it was humid, but not too bad, just like home. So he was like, hey, I have a good shot at this thing. When the race starts, everyone comes out blazing.
<v Jared Marcelle>伊恩·迪尔（Ian Dille）发生这一切时21岁。他正在参加一场大型自行车比赛，全国锦标赛。是在盖恩斯维尔。伊恩来自奥斯汀。当他们到达那里时，他发现道路相当平坦，就像家里一样。另外，天气潮湿，但还不错，就像家里一样。所以他心想，嘿，我有很好的机会赢。比赛开始时，每个人都火力全开。

00:48:56.960 --> 00:49:18.680
<v Ian Dille>Everybody was racing really aggressively in that race. There was just a lot of attacks. There'd be a lead group of five riders, and then it would become 10, and then 15, and 20. And then a group would come out of that five riders. And it was kind of like this amoeba that kept breaking up and coming back together.
<v Ian Dille>每个人在那场比赛中都竞争得非常积极。攻击很多。先是一个由五名车手组成的领先小组，然后变成了10名，然后是15名，20名。然后一个小组从那五名车手中脱颖而出。它就像是一个不断分裂又重组的变形虫。

00:49:18.680 --> 00:49:49.640
<v Jared  Marcelle>Ian wasn't considered one of the favorites, but on this day in this race, he was better than most. He kept attacking, taking the lead, staying near the front the whole time. And then at one point, he realizes, oh man, I'm first. Now you'd think being in first place is a good thing. But for the majority of a bike race, competitors play hot potato with the top spot. Chances are, if you stay in first early on for too long, you're probably going to lose.
<v Jared  Marcelle>伊恩并不被认为是热门选手之一，但在这一天的比赛中，他比大多数人都强。他不断进攻，取得领先，全程保持在前列。然后他意识到，噢天哪，我是第一。现在你可能会认为第一名是一件好事。但在自行车比赛的大部分时间里，竞争者们对领先位置玩着“热土豆”游戏。很有可能，如果你在早期保持第一名太久，你很可能会输。

00:49:49.640 --> 00:50:00.160
<v Jared  Marcelle>No, you're definitely going to lose, because the person in first takes on all the wind. In cycling, this is a big thing. The front-runner basically works the hardest.
<v Jared  Marcelle>不，你肯定会输，因为第一名要承担所有的风阻。在自行车运动中，这是一件大事。领跑者基本上是最辛苦的。

00:50:00.160 --> 00:50:08.000
<v Ian Dille>And that makes the person that's pedaling behind them have to work a lot less hard. I mean scientifically, it's like 30% to 50% less hard.
<v Ian Dille>而这使得跟在他们后面踩踏板的人不必那么费力。在科学上，这大约是少费30%到50%的力。

00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:13.640
<v Jared Marcelle>That's why in bike races, you see bikers lined up behind the lead guy.
<v Jared Marcelle>这就是为什么在自行车比赛中，你会看到骑手们排在领头人后面。

00:50:13.640 --> 00:50:27.030
<v Ian Dille>I remember bending my head over as I was pedaling as hard as I could, and looking behind me, and yeah. I just saw this kind of white-and-black checkered jersey coming across to me, and that was Mike.
<v Ian Dille>我记得我尽可能用力踩踏板时把头低下来，向后看，是的。我看到了这种黑白相间的格纹球衣越过我，那是迈克。

00:50:27.030 --> 00:50:35.070
<v Jared Marcelle>Mike Friedman. Ian knew who the favorites at this race were, and this Mike Friedman wasn't one of them.
<v Jared Marcelle>迈克·弗里德曼（Mike Friedman）。伊恩知道这场比赛的热门选手是谁，而这个迈克·弗里德曼并不在其中。

00:50:35.070 --> 00:50:51.240
<v Ian Dille>And so I just sprinted as hard as I could. He would claw his way back to me, and I just couldn't believe it. He seemed so done, but he was just really tenacious. He just kept coming back.
<v Ian Dille>所以我只是尽可能用力冲刺。他会竭尽全力追上我，我简直无法相信。他看起来已经精疲力竭了，但他真的非常顽强。他不断追赶上来。

00:50:51.240 --> 00:51:04.120
<v Jared Marcelle>Ian was worried now. If he kept the lead, blocking all of the wind for Mike, he was going to get tired. Mike might burst ahead and win. So Ian was like, let him get in front for a bit.
<v Jared Marcelle>伊恩现在很担心。如果他保持领先，挡住迈克的所有风阻，他就会累坏。迈克可能会冲刺并赢下比赛。所以伊恩心想，让他领先一会吧。

00:51:04.120 --> 00:51:22.690
<v Ian Dille>I started coasting, and then we were kind of just coasting. And he would look back, and the group would be getting closer to catching us. And he said, you can win.
<v Ian Dille>我开始滑行，然后我们基本上只是在滑行。他会向后看，大部队会离我们越来越近。他说，“你可以赢。”

00:51:22.690 --> 00:51:25.830
<v Jared Marcelle>You can win. Three words.
<v Jared Marcelle>“你可以赢。”三个词。

00:51:25.830 --> 00:51:42.270
<v Ian Dille>I knew instantly what it meant. He means, if I stop attacking him and let him stay with me, and let him sit in my draft, then when we get to the finish line, he won't sprint me. He'll let me win the bike race.
<v Ian Dille>我瞬间就知道那意味着什么。他的意思是，如果我停止攻击他，让他和我待在一起，让他坐在我的气流里，那么当我们到达终点线时，他不会冲刺超过我。他会让我赢得自行车比赛。

00:51:42.270 --> 00:52:06.510
<v Jared Marcelle>Now in cycling, this type of gentlemen's agreement happens all the time. Competitors will temporarily agree to a truce so they can conserve energy and stay ahead of the pack. It's a strategy. Sometimes this happens at the end, which could end up deciding who wins. Like hey, there's two of us here at the top. I won't sprint on you if you take on the wind resistance for me. We can beat everyone else. Ian went for it.
<v Jared Marcelle>在自行车运动中，这种绅士协定经常发生。竞争者们会暂时同意停战，这样他们就可以保存体力并保持在领头位置。这是一种策略。有时这发生在最后，这最终决定了谁获胜。比如，嘿，我们在顶端有两个人。如果你为我承担风阻，我就不会向你冲刺。我们可以击败其他人。伊恩同意了。

00:52:06.510 --> 00:52:42.110
<v Ian Dille>I just put my head down and went as hard as I could. I mean, I felt like I was just going so fast, and we had a motorcycle referee that would follow the race. And they come up and give you time splits. They'll either just tell you, or sometimes they'll have a whiteboard that they'll write the gap between you and the chasers. And so they kept coming up, and it would be, you have 20 seconds. And then it was like 30 seconds, and then 40 seconds, and the gap just kept going out, and out, and out.
<v Ian Dille>我只是低下头，尽我所能。我的意思是，我觉得我跑得太快了，我们有一名跟随比赛的摩托车裁判。他们会上来给你时间差。他们要么只是告诉你，有时他们会有一个白板，写下你和追赶者之间的差距。所以他们不断上来，说，你有20秒。然后是30秒，40秒，差距不断扩大，扩大，扩大。

00:52:42.110 --> 00:53:21.540
<v Ian Dille>I remember with one lap to go and just feeling so happy, like I'd already won the race. And then so we go around, and then we're coming towards the finish line. And we're getting ready to take the final left-hand turn to the finish. And I turned to him and I was like, yeah, you remember our deal, right? And then Mike just started sprinting. And I just remember watching him come by on my left, and my legs started cramping as we went up that hill.
<v Ian Dille>我记得在还剩一圈时感到非常开心，好像我已经赢得了比赛。然后我们绕过去，正朝着终点线前进。我们正准备进行向终点的最后一次左转。我转过去对他说了声，是的，你记得我们的交易，对吧？然后迈克就开始冲刺。我只记得看着他从我的左侧冲过去，当我们上那座山时，我的腿开始抽筋。

00:53:21.540 --> 00:53:32.850
<v Ian Dille>And I was just like, I can't believe this is happening. It felt like this dream just all of a sudden kind of turned into a nightmare. And I didn't win the bike race.
<v Ian Dille>我简直不敢相信这是真的。感觉这个梦突然变成了一场噩梦。我没有赢得自行车比赛。

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:34.810
<v Jared Marcelle>Mike did. Mike won.
<v Jared Marcelle>迈克赢了。迈克赢了。

00:53:34.810 --> 00:53:48.440
<v Ian Dille>Disbelief quickly turned to total rage. I was so upset. Remember riding up next to him, and hitting him on the back, and yelling something like, what the [BLEEP]?
<v Ian Dille>不相信很快变成了彻底的愤怒。我太难过了。记得骑到他旁边，打他的后背，并大喊类似，“什么鬼（[BLEEP]）？”的话。

00:53:48.440 --> 00:53:53.830
<v Jared Marcelle>First place takes home a jersey with stars and stripes, like Captain America.
<v Jared Marcelle>第一名带回一件有星星和条纹的球衣，就像美国队长一样。

00:53:53.830 --> 00:54:21.120
<v Ian Dille>I watched them put Mike's medal on, and then they gave him a jersey. And then, traditionally, everybody raises their arms on the podium after the national anthem plays. And I was standing right next to Mike, and I didn't-- I wouldn't hold his hand. I didn't raise my arm. And that was like the photo of the race.
<v Ian Dille>我看着他们给迈克戴上奖牌，然后他们给了他一件球衣。然后，按照传统，国歌播放后，每个人都会在领奖台上举起双臂。我当时就站在迈克旁边，我没有——我不会握他的手。我没有举起我的手臂。那成了这场比赛的照片。

00:54:21.120 --> 00:54:27.940
<v Jared  Marcelle>Ian was not having it. He immediately told the reporter what happened. He was like, Mike cheated. He cheated.
<v Jared  Marcelle>伊恩不打算接受这一点。他立即告诉记者发生了什么。他说，迈克作弊了。他作弊了。

00:54:27.940 --> 00:54:59.020
<v Ian Dille>His side of the story was that there was no deal, that I was just confused. When he said, you can win this race, he was like, no, no, no, no. What I was saying was if we work together, if you stop attacking me, you can win. Stop attacking me. Stop trying to get rid of me. You can win if you just keep pulling, and don't let this group behind catch us.
<v Ian Dille>他的故事版本是根本没有交易，我只是搞错了。当他说“你可以赢得这场比赛”时，他是说，不，不，不，不。我想说的是，如果我们合作，如果你停止攻击我，你就能赢。停止攻击我。停止试图摆脱我。如果你继续拉动，别让后面的人追上我们，你就能赢。

00:54:59.020 --> 00:55:34.510
<v Jared Marcelle>As if to say, hey, man, you're doing well. You keep it up, you might even win this thing. I said you could win, not that I'd let you win. So life goes on. Ian races professionally for a bit, but gets injured and becomes a journalist. Of course, he covers cycling. Mike becomes an Olympian. The whole time, they're sort of circling each other's orbits, but avoiding crossing paths. And annoyingly, everyone in the racing world really likes Mike.
<v Jared Marcelle>好像在说，嘿，伙计，你做得很好。你继续保持，甚至可能赢得比赛。我说的是你能赢，而不是我会让你赢。所以生活还要继续。伊恩在职业赛场待了一会儿，但受伤了，成为了一名记者。当然，他报道自行车运动。迈克成了奥运选手。在这期间，他们一直在彼此的轨道周围盘旋，但避免交集。而且烦人的是，赛车界的每个人都很喜欢迈克。

00:55:34.510 --> 00:56:06.640
<v Ian Dille>He had this nickname as like Meatball, and he had a blog, and there was a lot of love for Meatball. And I was a little bit resentful about that. I always thought, they don't know the real Mike. He's a cheater. And he goes back on his word. And I would tell people, or people would ask me about, what happened in that race? And I'd be like, well, Mike's a cheater. And they'd just be like, well, doesn't seem like something he would do. And then I'd be like, no, you don't know.
<v Ian Dille>他有个绰号叫“肉丸”（Meatball），他有个博客，人们对肉丸有很多喜爱。我对此感到有点怨恨。我一直认为，他们不知道真实的迈克。他是个骗子。他违背了他的承诺。我会告诉人们，或者人们会问我关于那场比赛发生了什么，我会说，好吧，迈克是个骗子。他们会说，好吧，这看起来不像他会做的事。然后我会说，不，你不了解。

00:56:06.640 --> 00:56:14.180
<v Ian Dille>People would sort of look at me sideways when I was like, I hate Mike Friedman. They'd be like, nobody hates Mike Friedman. What are you talking about?
<v Ian Dille>当我说我讨厌迈克·弗里德曼时，人们会斜眼看我。他们会说，没人讨厌迈克·弗里德曼。你在说什么？

00:56:14.180 --> 00:56:16.630
<v Jared Marcelle>That's like saying you hate Mr. Rogers, right?
<v Jared Marcelle>那就像说你讨厌罗杰斯先生一样，对吧？

00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:17.760
<v Ian Dille>Yeah. Yeah.
<v Ian Dille>是的。是的。

00:56:17.760 --> 00:56:23.680
<v Jared Marcelle>So as time passes, this race stays like a thorn in Ian's side.
<v Jared Marcelle>所以随着时间的推移，这场比赛一直像一根刺扎在伊恩心里。

00:56:23.680 --> 00:56:45.750
<v Ian Dille>I realize saying that sounds like a high school football player talking about competing in the state title game, or whatever else. And then I totally get how ridiculous and small that sounds as an adult. But it's weird. It's just weird how you cling to those things.
<v Ian Dille>我意识到这么说听起来像是一个高中橄榄球运动员在谈论参加州冠军赛或其他什么。我完全明白作为一个成年人这么说有多么荒谬和微小。但很奇怪。坚持那些事情真是奇怪。

00:56:45.750 --> 00:56:55.110
<v Jared Marcelle>Then one day, about 15 years later, Ian's covering a race in Colorado near where Mike lived. He's on the tour bus hanging out. And then--
<v Jared Marcelle>然后有一天，大约15年后，伊恩在科罗拉多州迈克家附近报道一场比赛。他在巡回巴士上闲逛。然后——

00:56:55.110 --> 00:57:18.220
<v Ian Dille>One of the guys on the bus was like, oh, I'm going to give Mike a call and see if he'll come to the start line and hang out with us on the bus before we start. And Mike came onto the bus. We didn't even look at each other, and then he left. And then when he came back, I was standing outside the bus. And he had a tray of coffees, and he handed me one.
<v Ian Dille>巴士上的一个人说，哦，我要给迈克打个电话，看看他能不能在我们开始前过来起点线跟我们在巴士上待一会儿。迈克上了巴士。我们甚至没看对方一眼，然后他就离开了。当他回来时，我正站在巴士外面。他端着一盘咖啡，给了我一杯。

00:57:18.220 --> 00:57:34.210
<v Ian Dille>And I think he was just like, I just want to talk to you about that day. I think about that race all the time. And I was like, man, me too.
<v Ian Dille>我想他只是说，我只想和你谈谈那天的事。我一直想着那场比赛。我说，伙计，我也是。

