<v Mike Friedman>And I remember seeing his lower lip shaking, just shaking.
<v Mike Friedman>我记得看到他的下唇在颤抖，在颤抖。

00:57:39.740 --> 00:57:52.050
<v Jared Marcelle>And that is Mike Friedman. That day on the bus, Mike didn't fully fess up and apologize. For Mike, it's complicated. Here's how he remembers those final moments of the race.
<v Jared Marcelle>那正是迈克·弗里德曼。在那天巴士上，迈克并没有完全坦白并道歉。对于迈克来说，这很复杂。这是他记忆中比赛最后时刻的样子。

00:57:52.050 --> 00:58:05.980
<v Mike Friedman>But I wasn't even thinking. I was just, man, we made this left-hand turn, and the finish line is right within sight, 250 meters. And before I knew it, I was sprinting. Even going up to the line, I knew what I was doing was wrong, but I couldn't stop.
<v Mike Friedman>但我当时根本没思考。我只是，伙计，我们完成了这个左转，终点线就在视线内，250米。在我意识到之前，我就在冲刺了。即使冲向终点线，我也知道我在做错事，但我停不下来。

00:58:05.980 --> 00:58:11.690
<v Jared  Marcelle>Do you know why you did what you did, or have a better understanding of why you did it?
<v Jared  Marcelle>你知道你为什么做你所做的事，或者更好地理解你为什么那么做吗？

00:58:11.690 --> 00:58:53.930
<v Mike Friedman>Well, I was 17 years old at the time. So I just-- yeah. I don't know. It just is kind of one of those things that I don't have an answer. You know, I try my best, but I don't always do my best kind of thing. It wasn't preplanned. It wasn't done in a way to be nefarious. It wasn't, I'm going to tell this guy I'm going to do this and then sprint. It wasn't done in that way. It wasn't done that way at all. That's it. You know, that's it.
<v Mike Friedman>好吧，我当时才17岁。所以我只是——是的。我不知道。这只是那种我没有答案的事情之一。你知道，我尽了最大努力，但我并不总是做到最好。这不是预谋的。它不是以那种邪恶的方式完成的。它不是，我要告诉这个人我要这样做，然后冲刺。它不是那样完成的。它根本不是那样完成的。就是这样。你知道，就是这样。

00:58:53.930 --> 00:58:58.520
<v Mike Friedman>That's the only time I've ever cheated, ever, at a bicycle race, ever.
<v Mike Friedman>这是我在自行车比赛中唯一一次，有史以来，唯一一次作弊。

00:58:58.520 --> 00:59:00.670
<v Jared Marcelle>He immediately regretted it.
<v Jared Marcelle>他立即后悔了。

00:59:00.670 --> 00:59:05.820
<v Jared  Marcelle>Did it change how you saw yourself as an athlete, as a competitor?
<v Jared  Marcelle>这改变了你作为一名运动员、作为一名竞争者的自我形象吗？

00:59:05.820 --> 00:59:21.270
<v Mike Friedman>It did change how I saw myself. This is just-- I don't have anything else like that, that I can definitively say affected me the way that this had. That was the one thing that I knew that I had done wrong.
<v Mike Friedman>它确实改变了我对自己的看法。这只是——除了这个，我没有其他任何事情可以明确地说像这样影响了我。这是我知道我做错的一件事。

00:59:21.270 --> 00:59:38.400
<v Jared Marcelle>It ate at him. He didn't race for two years. Mike didn't even tell anyone for a really long time. Fessing up that he cheated would mean that he was just that-- a cheater, not a kid who lost himself for a minute in a race.
<v Jared Marcelle>这一直在啃噬他。他有两年没比赛。迈克很长一段时间没告诉任何人。承认他作弊意味着他就是那样的人——一个骗子，而不是一个在比赛中迷失了一分钟的孩子。

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:50.660
<v Mike Friedman>Dude, I didn't admit that this happened until I was with someone that I wanted to marry. And then there was one other person that I told while I was drunk, and he's my best friend.
<v Mike Friedman>伙计，我直到和我想结婚的人在一起时才承认这件事发生过。然后还有一个人是我喝醉时告诉的，他是我最好的朋友。

00:59:50.660 --> 01:00:11.980
<v Jared Marcelle>It never met as much to anyone else as it did to him, but he couldn't let it go. He thought about it for years. After his cycling career was done, his life sort of tanked. His marriage ended, hit bottom. He was living in a camper on his friend's land when he realized he wanted to reach out to Ian, that he missed his opportunity on the bus a year back. Here's Ian.
<v Jared Marcelle>这件事对任何人来说都没有对他意义重大，但他无法释怀。他思考了多年。他的自行车生涯结束后，他的生活基本上陷入了低谷。他的婚姻结束了，跌到了谷底。他住在朋友土地上的露营车里，当时他意识到他想联系伊恩，他错过了之前一年在巴士上的机会。这是伊恩的观点。

01:00:11.980 --> 01:00:36.630
<v Ian Dille>He was moving back to Colorado, and he was driving back. And he called me, and that's when he said, yeah, you're right. It was a deal. And I'm sorry that I crossed the finish line first, that I didn't hold up my end of the bargain.
<v Ian Dille>他要搬回科罗拉多，正在开车回去。他给我打了电话，那时候他说，是的，你是对的。那是场交易。我很抱歉我第一个越过终点线，没有履行我的承诺。

01:00:36.630 --> 01:00:45.100
<v Jared Marcelle>Mike also wants to give Ian the jersey, the Captain America one. He'd stuffed it deep in a drawer all these years. So they agreed to meet up.
<v Jared Marcelle>迈克也想把球衣——那件美国队长的球衣——给伊恩。这些年来，他把它深深塞在抽屉里。所以他们同意见面。

01:00:45.100 --> 01:01:11.880
<v Ian Dille>He drives this really like revved-up-- I think it's a Jeep, or a pickup truck, or something. And you could hear it rumbling down the street, like rum-rum-rum-rum-rum. You could hear him pull up, and I was kind of nervous. And he came up, and he was holding the jersey, and he gave it to me right away. He looked me in the eyes, and he said, sorry. And I said, thank you.
<v Ian Dille>他开着一辆看起来非常轰鸣的——我想是吉普车，或者皮卡车之类的。你可以听到它在街上轰鸣，像“隆-隆-隆-隆-隆”。你可以听到他开过来，我有点紧张。他走过来，手里拿着球衣，立刻把它给了我。他看着我的眼睛说，“对不起。”我说，“谢谢。”

01:01:11.880 --> 01:01:44.510
<v Ian Dille>He had talked about, like I think he had even called the governing body, and asked them if there's any way that the actual results could be changed. And yeah. He was just like, every effort to make it right. I was just like, wow. When does that ever happen in someone's life? I don't even know when I've ever done that, just made something that I did wrong right, decades later.
<v Ian Dille>他谈到，我想他甚至打电话给管理机构，询问是否有任何方法可以更改实际结果。是的。他就像是，每一种努力都为了让它变得正确。我只是想，“哇”。这种情况在一个人的一生中何时会发生？我甚至不知道我什么时候做过那样的事，仅仅是纠正了我做错的事，几十年后。

01:01:44.510 --> 01:02:07.310
<v Jared  Marcelle>This event set off by a few words had just ballooned and ballooned in their heads for about 15 years, even though, in the grand scheme of things, this race didn't matter. It's not like it was the reason Mike made it, or the thing that ended Ian's career. And fixing it took something that's so simple-- three words. You were right.
<v Jared  Marcelle>这件由几句话引发的事件在他们脑海中膨胀了大约15年，尽管从宏观来看，这场比赛并不重要。它不像它是迈克成功的理由，或结束伊恩职业生涯的事情。而纠正它需要一些非常简单的事情——三个词。“你是对的。”

01:02:07.310 --> 01:02:47.630
<v Ira        Glass>Jared Marcelle. His reporting can be heard on the podcast Caught from WNYC. Ian Dille, the racer who got cheated out of his victory, wrote about this in Bicycle Magazine. That's how we heard about the story. The other racer, Mike Friedman, now runs a non-profit teaching kids about bikes, called Pedaling Minds.
<v Ira        Glass>杰瑞德·马塞尔。他的报道可以在WNYC的播客《Caught》中听到。伊恩·迪尔，那位被骗去胜利的赛车手，在《自行车杂志》上写了关于这件事的报道。这就是我们听到这个故事的方式。另一位赛车手，迈克·弗里德曼，现在经营着一个非营利组织，向孩子们传授自行车知识，叫《Pedaling Minds》。

01:02:47.630 --> 01:03:07.180
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Diane Wu. The people who put our show together includes Ben Calhoun, Zoe Chace, Dana Chivvis, Sean Cole, Aviva DeKornfeld, Neil Drumming, Jarrett Floyd, Damien Graef, Miki Meek, Stowe Nelson, Katherine Raimando, Ben Feiglin, Nadia Reiman, Robyn Semien, Alissa Ship, Lilly Sullivan, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, and Nancy Updike. Our managing editors are Susan Burton and David Kestenbaum.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目由黛安·吴（Diane Wu）制作。组成我们节目的团队包括：本·卡尔霍恩（Ben Calhoun）、佐伊·蔡斯（Zoe Chace）、达纳·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis）、肖恩·科尔（Sean Cole）、阿维娃·德科恩菲尔德（Aviva DeKornfeld）、尼尔·德拉明（Neil Drumming）、贾勒特·弗洛伊德（Jarrett Floyd）、达米安·格拉夫（Damien Graef）、米基·米克（Miki Meek）、斯托·纳尔逊（Stowe Nelson）、凯瑟琳·雷曼多（Katherine Raimando）、本·费格林（Ben Feiglin）、纳迪亚·雷曼（Nadia Reiman）、罗宾·塞米恩（Robyn Semien）、阿丽莎·希普（Alissa Ship）、莉莉·沙利文（Lilly Sullivan）、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala）、马特·蒂尔尼（Matt Tierney）和南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）。我们的执行编辑是苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton）和大卫·克滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）。

01:03:07.180 --> 01:03:14.370
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks to Dave Ptolemy Slocum, Chuck Long, Chris Bateman, David Goldenberg, Veronica Simmons, Astrid Lange, Brendan Copley, and Michelle Solomon.
<v Ira Glass>特别感谢戴夫·托勒密·斯洛克姆（Dave Ptolemy Slocum）、查克·朗（Chuck Long）、克里斯·贝特曼（Chris Bateman）、大卫·戈尔登伯格（David Goldenberg）、维罗妮卡·西蒙斯（Veronica Simmons）、阿斯特丽德·兰格（Astrid Lange）、布伦丹·科普利（Brendan Copley）和米歇尔·所罗门（Michelle Solomon）。

01:03:14.370 --> 01:03:35.020
<v Ira Glass>Our website, thisamericanlife.org, where you can listen to our archive of over 600 episodes for absolutely free. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange. Thanks, as always, to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, I called him earlier today. When I called him, I don't know, he was at the gym in the middle of his sprint intervals.
<v Ira Glass>我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以免费收听我们超过600集的档案。《美国生活》由公共广播交易所 PRX 分发到公共广播电台。像往常一样，感谢我们节目的联合创始人托雷·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生。你知道，我今天早些时候给他打了电话。当我打电话给他时，不知道，他正在健身房进行短跑间歇训练。

01:03:35.020 --> 01:03:40.640
<v Jennifer  Lemesurier>I mean, I can't really-- like, I mean, man, the timing of this.
<v Jennifer  Lemesurier>我是说，我真的无法——我是说，伙计，这真是太巧了。

01:03:40.640 --> 01:03:45.640
<v Ira Glass>Actually, real-life Torey just had back surgery this week. Feel better, OK? I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>其实，现实生活中的托雷这周刚做了背部手术。早日康复，好吗？我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周再见，会有更多《美国生活》的故事。
